# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored; this template is not.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Every value below is generated by you — none is issued by a service, and
# nothing outside this deployment needs to agree with them. Generate each
# once and keep it: regenerating CDE_JWT_SECRET invalidates every session
# token, and regenerating CDE_STORAGE_SIGNING_KEY invalidates every download
# URL already handed out.
#
# Deliberately empty. A template that ships a working default is how a
# development key reaches production, and how the key this project used to
# carry ended up in the git history permanently.

# Signs the bearer tokens, including the tenant claim that Row-Level Security
# reads. Anyone holding this can mint a token for any tenant, so it is the
# highest-value secret here. At least 32 bytes; the application refuses to
# start on a shorter one.
#   openssl rand -base64 48
CDE_JWT_SECRET=

# Signs the time-limited download URLs issued by the storage layer.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
CDE_STORAGE_SIGNING_KEY=

# Read by docker compose to initialise Postgres. If you point at a database
# that already exists, this must match the password it was created with
# rather than being generated fresh — Postgres only reads it on first
# initialisation of the volume, so changing it later has no effect until
# `docker compose down -v`.
#   openssl rand -base64 24
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# Only needed when the application runs on the host (`./gradlew bootRun`)
# against the compose database. Under full compose the application gets this
# from docker-compose.yml and you can leave it blank.
#
# application.yml defaults it to `cde`, which will not match the
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD above, so the host path fails password authentication
# without it. Set it to the same value:
#   SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD=<same as POSTGRES_PASSWORD>
SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD=

# ── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Required only when the feature they belong to is switched on; the
# application starts happily without them.

# Encrypts TOTP secrets at rest. Required when cde.mfa.enabled is true.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# CDE_MFA_SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Seeds one administrator at startup so a development stack can be logged
# into without registering an account first. Uncomment both lines to get a
# standing local account instead of a fresh registration every time.
#
# Generate the password once and leave it in this file. It persists — the
# seeder writes the account on the first start against an empty database and
# then leaves it alone, so the credential stays valid for the life of the
# `pgdata` volume, across restarts and rebuilds.
#
# Choose a value here rather than expecting one: nothing in the repository
# supplies a fallback, and the application refuses to start if seeding is on
# without one, if it is under 12 characters, or if it is the password this
# seeder used to hard-code. Keep it to this deployment — .env is gitignored,
# and the account is a real ADMIN, not a reduced-privilege test user.
#   openssl rand -base64 24
# CDE_SEED_ENABLED=true
# CDE_SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Required when the AI features are enabled for a tenant. See §10.1 before
# switching this on for a government or Defence tenant — it is off by default
# for those tiers deliberately.
# CDE_AI_API_KEY=
